From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 09:18:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Make each IndexedDB transaction one SQL
 transaction
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Each batch of requests ran in a storage transaction of its own, so the
requests of one IndexedDB transaction that reached the backend in
different messages were committed separately, as they arrived. An abort
only dropped the requests that hadn't run yet (the TODO in abort()): a
put that had replied survived the abort of its transaction, the bug
found in the review of servo-ohos PR #2. After an aborted upgrade the
manager wrote the old version, object stores, indexes and key generator
numbers back from snapshots, and it restored key generators the same
way after an aborted readwrite transaction.

Now a readwrite or versionchange transaction is one SQL transaction on
the database's connection (the actor of the previous change):

- Its first request begins it with BEGIN IMMEDIATE, and it stays open
  across batches until script commits or aborts. The commit protocol
  stays (Commit, TxnMaybeCommit, RequestHandled); the manager hands the
  commit to the engine, whose COMMIT result goes to the commit
  callbacks, so the complete event only follows a successful COMMIT. A
  failed COMMIT reports its error, and script aborts the transaction.
- An abort is a ROLLBACK, which undoes the transaction's records, its
  key generator numbers and, for an upgrade, its schema operations and
  the version change; the abort callbacks are sent once it is done. The
  snapshots and the restore code are gone (KeyGeneratorSnapshot,
  VersionUpgrade.object_stores, restore_object_stores,
  restore_key_generators_after_abort), and with them the engine's
  metadata writes outside a transaction. Script's own revert of its
  metadata stays.
- Readonly requests run without a transaction of their own, inside the
  open SQL transaction if there is one: the scheduler never runs them
  alongside a writer with an overlapping scope.
- Every request replies as soon as it has run.
- Writers of one database start one at a time, also with disjoint
  scopes, since each is the one SQL transaction of the connection. The
  spec allows it, and Firefox does the same. A writer that hasn't
  started blocks no one.
- A write or schema operation that fails in SQL fails its transaction:
  it is rolled back at once, and its later requests and its commit fail
  with the error, so script aborts it (there are no savepoints, since
  RDB rejects RELEASE). The request replies with the same error its
  transaction records, so a full disk is a QuotaExceededError both
  ways. After every job on the connection, and before a writer's
  request begins, the engine checks whether the open SQL transaction
  ended, as SQLite's own rollback after SQLITE_FULL does and the
  executors' after the errors that may have: then the writer has
  failed, also when the statement that ended it wasn't the writer's
  own, such as a readonly request or a metadata read of the manager
  running inside the writer's transaction.
- An upgrade transaction writes the new version when its SQL
  transaction begins, at its first request or at its commit, rather
  than when the manager sets the upgrade up, where a writer of a
  connection that was closed but not finished could still hold the
  connection's transaction.

KvsEngine follows: batches carry their transaction, and it gains
upgrade_version, commit and rollback, while its metadata writers go.
The manager's UpgradeVersion message, which nothing sends, now replies
with an error instead of writing the version.

Tests on every executor: an abort rolls back data, schema operations
and the version across batches; a transaction over many batches is
invisible to another connection until it commits, and an abort leaves
nothing; requests reply in the order they were handed over, across a
writer and readonly transactions; two databases progress independently
while one actor is busy; a failed write, a failed begin, a statement
that ends the SQL transaction and a failed commit each fail the
transaction; a readonly request, another job on the connection or a
manager read that ends the SQL transaction fails the writer; an upgrade
without requests sets the version at its commit, and one that aborts
leaves it alone; a write transaction holds across idle minutes
(ignored, about three minutes). The manager's tests check an aborted
upgrade, an aborted key generator, a failed commit, that only a
started writer holds back other writers, that a readonly transaction
waits for an earlier overlapping writer, and a readwrite transaction
without requests; the integration tests an aborted put that had
replied, writers with disjoint scopes, and an aborted upgrade that
deleted a store.

WPT: two subtests per engine pass now, in both globals: "Transactions
that explicitly commit and have errors should abort." in
idb-explicit-commit, and "Ensure that databases() doesn't pick up
changes that haven't commited." in get-databases. The latter passes
because its upgrade sends no request before calling databases(), so the
version isn't written yet; databases() still reads uncommitted state.
idbtransaction-oncomplete, which P0 had made flaky and P0's in-order
batches had fixed, stays fixed.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits` at this commit: 97 passed, 1 skipped (the ignored long test; before: 83). DAYU200 at the tip of the series, `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage -- [--no-default-features] --features ohos-rdb-backend --status-level all`: RDB only 106 passed, 3 skipped; both backends 182 passed, 4 skipped (the skips are the documented test_new_storage_threads_create_independent_groups and the ignored long tests). The ignored long tests alone on the board, before the review fixes (`… -- --config-file <nextest.toml with slow-timeout 60s × 10> --no-default-features --features ohos-rdb-backend --run-ignored only -E 'test(/transaction_(stays_open|holds)_across_idle_minutes/)'`): 2 passed in 182.9 s. `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` warning-free with SQLite only, RDB only and both. Without the check for an ended SQL transaction, without serial writers or without the rollback on abort, new tests fail. WPT IndexedDB/ at the tip of the series (with the P1 review fixes), passing subtests of 4,511: host SpiderMonkey 3,251 with no unexpected result, host QuickJS 3,253, DAYU200 in the board configuration (QuickJS + RDB) 3,247 (before the series 3,224, 3,229 and 3,222); this commit's new passes are the four subtests named above, in all three, 10 of 10 repeats on both host engines when first measured; no new failures: QuickJS idbversionchangeevent.any.sharedworker timed out once and passed 20 of 20 on both host engines, and the board's idbversionchangeevent.any.worker saw a blocked event from the sharedworker variant, which uses the same database name and whose connection deleteDatabase doesn't close yet; the other differences are known flips. idbtransaction-oncomplete, window and worker, `--repeat 30 --processes 4`, at the tip: 60 of 60.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent designed and wrote the transaction model and its tests, ran the unit tests and WPT on the host and the DAYU200, and fixed the P1 review's findings on ended SQL transactions, request errors and UpgradeVersion, with the scheduler and upgrade tests)
